{"author":{"id":"b409816c5cd5ab140d1d52eb33aace35de44952bb794b19806fd57f6ecb1ace9"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1791561270,"nonce":"B/wLPWw4XHEKP7yPeUXVAr1De/A=","title":"Tiered: each load() re-scans every function entry to list loaded modules","message":"Every module compiled into a running program refreshes the runtime's error-flag slots: `TieredRuntime::refresh_error_flags` asks `TieredBackend::error_flag_slots`, which calls `loaded_modules()`. That walks `current_module`, `loaded`, and then every `FunctionEntry` in `functions`, deduplicating each module by a linear `out.iter().any(ptr::eq)`. The walk grows with every function and every module already loaded, so a program that calls `load` repeatedly pays a cost proportional to everything loaded so far on each call.\n\nofficial/constructs.lua with the conformance prelude (`_U=true _soft=true _port=true _nomsg=true`) runs its short-circuit section through thousands of `load` calls. At d6c594565db161b4c37c63393a5b7b9d19a4a09a on the Mac (M-series, release, LLVM feature built, LLVM unselected) it takes 148 s wall / 86 s user; lua5.4 takes well under a second. The conformance harness's 120 s per-file deadline fails it under `cargo test` load (`warm_up::official` reported `official/constructs.lua` timed out). A 10 s `sample` of the run: 7690 samples in `host_load`, 4954 in `refresh_error_flags`, 4722 in `loaded_modules`.\n\n`error_flag_slots` also rebuilds the whole slot list from every loaded module's globals on each load, which stays proportional to the program's size even with a constant-time module walk.","files":null}]}